From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Quarrier <squarrier@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 16:08:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] cuda/exl3 + glm5_next/cuda: MiaAI-Lab's EXL3 prompt kernel
 for GLM's prompt chunks, TF_GLM_EXL3_PROMPT (MiaAI-Lab recipe patches 0004,
 0009, 0020)

MiaAI-Lab's recipe (github.com/MiaAI-Lab/GLM-5.3-Flash-EXL3-2x-DGX-Sparks-TensorFold @ 1576746) runs GLM-5.3-Flash's
routed experts of a prompt chunk through dedicated prompt kernels: per pass of up to 64 (or 128) members of one expert,
Y^T = W^T X^T with each output one fp32 chain of mma over ascending k tiles (no split K, no partials in memory), each
decoded trellis tile feeding the whole pass, and the gate/up and down epilogues fused. A row's bits depend on its own
values only, so any chunking gives the same prompt state; they are the recipe's prompt bits, not the grouped kernel's
(which sums split and warp partials). This ports the EXL3 part of recipe patch 0004-glm-prompt-kernels at the end state
of the recipe's chain (with 0009-glm-prefill-kernels' shared-row inputs and 0020-glm-prompt-experts-order's item
order), Apache License 2.0, Copyright 2026 MiaAI-Lab:

- cuda/exl3/experts_prompt.cu (new): the recipe's helpers (mcg2, decode_tile_p, mma16816, fwht128, bf16r, cp.async,
  ldmatrix), pass configuration, prompt_mainloop, prompt_spill, prompt_smem, prompt_order_kernel and prompt_launch
  byte for byte, and its gate/up and down kernels with data movement changed only: a kernel reads its expert's
  trellis base from the layer's per-expert pointer table and runs the recipe's main loop on it; a member is the
  universal grouping's code row * 32 + slot, read as the recipe plan's pair row * slots + slot. prompt_plan_kernel
  (new) lays group()'s grouping out as the recipe plan's items (expert, first member, count) of up to a pass's
  members: the items the recipe's plan_kernel makes for the routed experts, in the same order.
- cuda/exl3/experts.cpp: the prompt binding. cuda/exl3/experts.py: routed(.., prompt, prompt_pass) runs a window
  through it (the recipe's rotations: rot_rows for a layer with a shared_suh under rowrot, else rot_in; then the plan,
  the recipe's item order under order, the kernels; Y combined by combine's fma chain when weights are given), for
  4-bit mcg layers at GLM's tile settings, widths in the kernels' blocks, 16-byte aligned trellises and GLM's bf16
  SwiGLU (prompt_kernel_ready); anything else launches as before. prompt_items gives the launch's items bound (the
  recipe's max_items); the plan buffers are made on a scratch's first prompt window. The extension is renamed
  tensorfold_exl3_experts_v4 (a new source).
- families/glm5_next/cuda/exl3_generic.py: prompt_switches reads the recipe's names: TF_GLM_EXL3_PROMPT (1 on; unset or
  0 off; anything else refuses), TF_GLM_EXL3_PASS (64, the recipe's default, or 128: the same bits),
  TF_GLM_EXL3_ORDER and TF_GLM_EXL3_ROWROT (on unless 0). routed(.., prompt=) adds them for a prompt chunk's window.
- families/glm5_next/cuda/experts_node.py: the expert node marks its MODE_CHUNK, MODE_SLICE and MODE_PAIR forwards as
  prompt chunks and compute passes the mark (decode windows never take the kernel); serve() reads the switch before
  the load and prints a startup line when it is on.

TF_GLM_EXL3_PROMPT is off unless set (the recipe's code turns it on). Unset, every call is the one before: GLM's call
sites pass the same arguments, no existing CUDA source changed, and the only change on that path is the extension's
name. Set, a prompt row's routed-expert outputs change class (the recipe's prompt bits). The new CUDA source needs to
be in the experts extension wherever the extensions are prebuilt (the module's _ext lists it).

Tests: tests/test_exl3_prompt_kernel.py (CPU: a model of prompt_plan_kernel gives exactly the recipe plan_kernel's
routed items, members and order at 1-2,048 rows, uniform and skewed routing, both passes, within prompt_items; the
recipe's order kernel (modelled) gives the same launch order on both plans; the switch's names, defaults and refusals;
unset, every GLM call is the call before; set, prompt chunks of 1-2,048 rows call the rotation and the kernel with the
expected buffers, decode windows and other callers do not, and layers outside the kernel's scope launch as before; the
expert node marks prompt chunks from the forward's mode). tests/cuda/test_exl3_prompt_kernel.py (GPU: against the
recipe's own kernel built from its tree (TF_MIA_TREE, pinned by sha256): xd and Y byte-equal at 1-2,048 rows, both
passes, item order on and off, rotation per row and per pair, shared and per-expert suh; the same bytes twice, and a
window's rows equal to the same rows in two smaller windows; times and the distance from the grouped kernel's bits
reported for information).

Credit: THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md ("GLM EXL3 prompt kernel (TF_GLM_EXL3_PROMPT)") and the kernel file's header name
MiaAI-Lab, the patches and the recipe commit.

